### Evict Entity from Hibernate Session

Source: https://github.com/hibernate/hibernate-orm/blob/main/documentation/src/main/asciidoc/userguide/chapters/pc/PersistenceContext.adoc

Use `Session.evict()` to remove an entity and its collections from the first-level cache, useful for memory management with large object graphs.

```java
session.evict(person);
```

--------------------------------

### Clear Persistence Context

Source: https://github.com/hibernate/hibernate-orm/blob/main/documentation/src/main/asciidoc/userguide/chapters/pc/PersistenceContext.adoc

Use `EntityManager.clear()` or `Session.clear()` to detach all entities from the current persistence context.

```java
entityManager.clear();
```

```java
session.clear();
```

--------------------------------

### Efficient Entity Insertion with Flush and Clear

Source: https://github.com/hibernate/hibernate-orm/blob/main/documentation/src/main/asciidoc/userguide/chapters/batch/Batching.adoc

Shows the recommended method for inserting a large number of entities by regularly flushing and clearing the session. This controls the size of the first-level cache, preventing memory exhaustion.

```java
final Session session = openSession();

try {
    final Transaction tx = session.beginTransaction();
    for ( int i = 0; i < 100000; i++ ) {
        Person person = new Person( "person " + i );
        session.persist( person );
        if ( i % 20 == 0 ) {
            session.flush();
            session.clear();
        }
    }
    tx.commit();
} finally {
    session.close();
}
```

--------------------------------

### Entity Identity Scope within a Hibernate Session

Source: https://github.com/hibernate/hibernate-orm/blob/main/documentation/src/main/asciidoc/userguide/chapters/domain/entity.adoc

Demonstrates how Hibernate guarantees that multiple requests for the same persistent entity within a single Session will return the exact same Java instance.

```java
include::{example-dir-mapping}/identifier/SimpleEntityTest.java[tag=entity-pojo-identity-scope-example, indent=0]
```

### Interacting with the database > Persistence contexts

Source: https://github.com/hibernate/hibernate-orm/blob/main/documentation/src/main/asciidoc/introduction/Interacting.adoc

A persistence context, also known as the first-level cache, holds a unique mapping of entity identifiers to entity instances that have been read or made persistent within its scope. Entity instances can be in one of three states: transient (never persistent), persistent (associated with the context), or detached (previously persistent but not currently associated). An entity instance can be associated with at most one persistence context at a time.

